    "body": "At Mailchimp, we have certain tables that contain sensitive information. In order to access this information safely, we have a few options:\r\n* Instead of accessing the table directly, use a predefined view that filters out sensitive information\r\n* If using the table directly, use a recommended set of filters, e.g. \r\n\r\n```\r\nWHERE\r\n  LOWER(TRIM(email)) NOT IN (\r\n  SELECT\r\n    email\r\n  FROM\r\n    opted_out_emails)\r\n```\r\n\r\nInitially, I think we'd simply check each query individually. In that case, I think it'd be great if we were able to implement these checks as classes similar to those in `src/sqlfluff/rules`. I was thinking a plug-in architecture could be good for this.\r\n\r\nFarther in the future, I could imagine doing more sophisticated analysis, such as looking across queries (either CTEs within a single query, or separate queries). In this case, I'm not sure how that would work, but perhaps we'd want to just use SQLFluff as a library, not using the command line. There is _potentially_ a tool idea there as well -- having a tool to output the parse tree in a neutral format like JSON, etc.",
